.he "dup2 (2)                     10/20/26                    dup2 (2)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
dup2
- duplicate a file descriptor onto a chosen number


.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
dup2(descriptor, descriptor2)
.br
int descriptor, descriptor2;


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Dup2
makes descriptor2 a synonym for descriptor, which must have
been returned by
.bd
open, creat,
or
.bd
pipe.
If descriptor2 is already open, it is closed first.
Subsequent reads or writes with descriptor2 have exactly the
same effect as the same call with descriptor.

Since
.bd
dup
always allocates the lowest available descriptor,
.bd
dup2
is the direct way to move a descriptor onto the standard
input or output without disturbing another one.  If the two
descriptors are the same, nothing happens and the call
succeeds.

.bd
Dup2
is subject to the limit of 16 active file descriptors
per process.


.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
.bd
Dup2()
returns descriptor2 if successful, or a -1 if not.


.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(dup2 = 45)
.br
.bd
(old descriptor in hl)
.br
.bd
sys; dup2; descriptor2
.br
.bd
(new descriptor in hl)


.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
On success, the carry flag is cleared and descriptor2
is returned in register hl.  On failure, the carry flag is set.
